window.CHTML=window.CHTML||{};window.CHTML.cb=window.CHTML.cb||{};window.CHTML.cb[19]={"h":"<h1 data-b=\"1\" id=\"b1\">Prerequisites for Phase 4: the Algebra and Asymptotics You Need</h1>\n<div class=\"box keybox\"><div class=\"bt\">What this chapter is</div>\n<p data-b=\"2\"> Not a reading summary: a <em>toolkit</em>. Chapters 20–25 use only the ideas collected here. Every result is derived or its derivation is pointed to; the derivations are mine, written from standard econometric theory, and match the treatment in Wooldridge (2010), Chapters 2–4 and 10 (the chapters that precede the ones you were assigned). Skim sections you know; the self-test below tells you which.</p>\n</div>\n<h2 data-b=\"3\" id=\"b3\">Self-test: can you skip a section?</h2>\n<table data-b=\"4\">\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>If you can already…</strong></th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Skip</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Show that <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>Z</mi><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow></math> is symmetric and idempotent and say what <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">19.2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Explain why <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">plim</mtext><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mrow><mi>O</mi><mi>L</mi><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Cov</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>/</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Var</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">19.5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">State the Slutsky theorem and the delta method</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">19.4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Derive the within estimator and say why it needs strict exogeneity</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">19.6</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 data-b=\"5\" id=\"b5\">Matrix algebra used throughout</h2>\n<h5 data-b=\"6\" id=\"b6\">Notation.</h5>\n<p data-b=\"7\">A column vector is <math display=\"inline\"><mi>x</mi></math>; its transpose is <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow></math>. <math display=\"inline\"><mi>X</mi></math> is <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> (rows are observations). <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></math> is the identity. <math display=\"inline\"><mi>A</mi></math> is <strong>symmetric</strong> if <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow></math>; it is <strong>positive definite</strong> (p.d.) if <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for every <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <strong>positive semidefinite</strong> (p.s.d.) if <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. For symmetric <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> we write <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>≽</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> if <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> is p.s.d.; this is what “one estimator is at least as efficient as another” means for variance matrices.</p>\n<h5 data-b=\"8\" id=\"b8\">Rank and inverses.</h5>\n<p data-b=\"9\"><math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">rank</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> is the number of linearly independent columns. If <math display=\"inline\"><mi>X</mi></math> (<math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>) has rank <math display=\"inline\"><mi>k</mi></math> then <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> is p.d. and invertible. <em>Proof:</em> <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"false\">∥</mo><mi>X</mi><mi>c</mi><msup><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"false\">∥</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, and it is zero only if <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, which rank <math display=\"inline\"><mi>k</mi></math> rules out unless <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h5 data-b=\"10\" id=\"b10\">Projection.</h5>\n<p data-b=\"11\">For a full-column-rank <math display=\"inline\"><mi>Z</mi></math> define <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>Z</mi><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</p>\n<div class=\"box thm\">\n<p data-b=\"12\"><strong>Result 19.1 (Projection matrices).</strong> <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub></math> are symmetric and idempotent (<math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub></mrow></math>); <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>; <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">rank</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">tr</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">rank</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"proof\">\n<p data-b=\"13\"><em>Proof.</em> Symmetry: <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>′</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub></mrow></math> since <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi></mrow></math> is symmetric. Idempotence: <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>Z</mi><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>Z</mi><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub></mrow></math>. <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>Z</mi><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></math>, hence <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mi>Z</mi><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Trace: <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">tr</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">tr</mtext><mo form=\"prefix\" maxsize=\"1.2\" minsize=\"1.2\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>Z</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>Z</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" maxsize=\"1.2\" minsize=\"1.2\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">tr</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> using <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">tr</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">tr</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mi>A</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>; an idempotent matrix has eigenvalues 0 or 1, so its trace is its rank. ◻</p>\n</div>\n<p data-b=\"14\"><em>Meaning:</em> <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is the vector of fitted values from regressing <math display=\"inline\"><mi>y</mi></math> on <math display=\"inline\"><mi>Z</mi></math>; it is the point of the column space of <math display=\"inline\"><mi>Z</mi></math> closest to <math display=\"inline\"><mi>y</mi></math>. Two-stage least squares (Chapter 20) is built on exactly this object.</p>\n<h5 data-b=\"15\" id=\"b15\">Minimising a quadratic form.</h5>\n<p data-b=\"16\">For a p.s.d. weighting matrix <math display=\"inline\"><mi>W</mi></math>, the minimiser of <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>X</mi><mi>b</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>′</mi><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>X</mi><mi>b</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> solves the first-order condition <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>X</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>X</mi><mi>b</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> (derivative of <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> is <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>A</mi><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> for symmetric <math display=\"inline\"><mi>A</mi></math>), so <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>b</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>W</mi><mi>X</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>X</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>W</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> if <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>W</mi><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> is invertible. This is the algebra behind both OLS (<math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></math>) and GMM (Chapter 21).</p>\n<h5 data-b=\"17\" id=\"b17\">The Kronecker product.</h5>\n<p data-b=\"18\">Wooldridge writes <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>⊗</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> for “the same instruments in every equation”. <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊗</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> replaces each entry <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></math> of <math display=\"inline\"><mi>A</mi></math> by the block <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>; for <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow></math> this is a block-diagonal matrix with <math display=\"inline\"><mi>B</mi></math> repeated <math display=\"inline\"><mi>G</mi></math> times. Nothing more is needed here.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"19\" id=\"b19\">Probability tools</h2>\n<h5 data-b=\"20\" id=\"b20\">Expectation, variance, covariance.</h5>\n<p data-b=\"21\"><math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Var</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>x</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>x</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>′</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Cov</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>x</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>y</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>′</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></mrow></math>. If <math display=\"inline\"><mi>a</mi></math> is a constant vector, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Var</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>x</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>′</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Var</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h5 data-b=\"22\" id=\"b22\">Conditional expectation and the law of iterated expectations (LIE).</h5>\n<p data-b=\"23\"><math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> is the best predictor of <math display=\"inline\"><mi>y</mi></math> given <math display=\"inline\"><mi>x</mi></math> in the mean-squared sense. LIE: <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></mrow></math> and, more generally, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mi>E</mi><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mi>x</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></mrow></math>. Corollary used repeatedly: <em>if <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> then <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></em> (because <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>). The converse is false: uncorrelated does not imply mean-independent. Many econometric statements (“exogenous”, “predetermined”) are statements of this kind; their strength differs, and Chapter 23 depends on the difference.</p>\n<h5 data-b=\"24\" id=\"b24\">Linear projection.</h5>\n<p data-b=\"25\">The linear projection of <math display=\"inline\"><mi>y</mi></math> on <math display=\"inline\"><mi>x</mi></math> is <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>β</mi></mrow></math> with <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>x</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>y</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> (Wooldridge 2010, Section 2.3). It exists whenever <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>x</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> is invertible and is defined by <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>′</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>β</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. It equals the conditional expectation only when the latter is linear.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"26\" id=\"b26\">Asymptotics</h2>\n<h5 data-b=\"27\" id=\"b27\">Convergence in probability.</h5>\n<p data-b=\"28\"><math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>θ</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>p</mi></mover><mi>θ</mi></mrow></math> (<math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">plim</mtext><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mi>θ</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></math>) means <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><msub><mover><mi>θ</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for every <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. An estimator with this property is <strong>consistent</strong>.</p>\n<h5 data-b=\"29\" id=\"b29\">Weak law of large numbers (WLLN).</h5>\n<p data-b=\"30\">If <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math> are i.i.d. with <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>∞</mi></mrow></math>, then <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>p</mi></mover><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h5 data-b=\"31\" id=\"b31\">Central limit theorem (CLT).</h5>\n<p data-b=\"32\">If <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math> are i.i.d. with mean <math display=\"inline\"><mi>μ</mi></math> and finite variance <math display=\"inline\"><mi>Σ</mi></math>, then <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>d</mi></mover><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Normal</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>Σ</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h5 data-b=\"33\" id=\"b33\">Slutsky and continuous mapping.</h5>\n<p data-b=\"34\">If <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>A</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>p</mi></mover><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><mi>g</mi></math> is continuous at <math display=\"inline\"><mi>A</mi></math>, then <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mover><mi>A</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>p</mi></mover><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>. In particular <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mover><mi>A</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>p</mi></mover><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> if <math display=\"inline\"><mi>A</mi></math> is invertible. If <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>A</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>p</mi></mover><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>d</mi></mover><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></math> then <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>A</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>d</mi></mover><mi>A</mi><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h5 data-b=\"35\" id=\"b35\">The delta method.</h5>\n<p data-b=\"36\">If <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mover><mi>θ</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>d</mi></mover><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Normal</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><mi>h</mi></math> is continuously differentiable with Jacobian <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>∂</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>/</mi><mi>∂</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow></math>, then <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mover><mi>θ</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>d</mi></mover><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Normal</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mi>V</mi><mi>H</mi><mi>′</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>. <em>Proof sketch:</em> mean-value expansion <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mover><mi>θ</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mover><mi>θ</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mover><mi>θ</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> and Slutsky.</p>\n<h5 data-b=\"37\" id=\"b37\">Mean-value expansion.</h5>\n<p data-b=\"38\">The workhorse of Chapter 22: if <math display=\"inline\"><mi>f</mi></math> is differentiable, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mover><mi>θ</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>f</mi><mi>′</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mover><mi>θ</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mover><mi>θ</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> for some <math display=\"inline\"><mover><mi>θ</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover></math> between them, and <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>θ</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>p</mi></mover><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> whenever <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>θ</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>p</mi></mover><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>.</p>\n<div class=\"box keybox\"><div class=\"bt\">Fixed T, large N</div>\n<p data-b=\"39\"> In every panel-data result of this phase, <strong><math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>∞</mi></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><mi>T</mi></math> is fixed</strong>. A bank panel with 12 banks and 15 years is not large-<math display=\"inline\"><mi>N</mi></math> by any stretch; Chapter 26 returns to what that means for your thesis.</p>\n</div>\n<h2 data-b=\"40\" id=\"b40\">OLS: the derivation you will reuse</h2>\n<p data-b=\"41\">Model <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> with <math display=\"inline\"><mi>x</mi></math> a <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>×</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></math> row. Stack <math display=\"inline\"><mi>N</mi></math> observations: <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>X</mi><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Minimising the sum of squares gives the normal equations <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi>′</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>X</mi><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so <math display=\"block\"><mrow><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>X</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>X</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>X</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>X</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mo form=\"prefix\" maxsize=\"1.8\" minsize=\"1.8\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo form=\"postfix\" maxsize=\"1.8\" minsize=\"1.8\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo form=\"prefix\" maxsize=\"1.8\" minsize=\"1.8\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" maxsize=\"1.8\" minsize=\"1.8\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>.</mi></mrow></math></p>\n<div class=\"box thm\">\n<p data-b=\"42\"><strong>Result 19.2 (Consistency and asymptotic normality of OLS).</strong> If (i) <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>u</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and (ii) <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>x</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> is invertible, with i.i.d. data and finite moments, then <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>p</mi></mover><mi>β</mi></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>d</mi></mover><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Normal</mtext><mo form=\"prefix\" maxsize=\"1.2\" minsize=\"1.2\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>B</mi><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo form=\"postfix\" maxsize=\"1.2\" minsize=\"1.2\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> with <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>x</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>x</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>x</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"proof\">\n<p data-b=\"43\"><em>Proof.</em> By the WLLN, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>p</mi></mover><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>p</mi></mover><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>′</mi><mi>u</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>; by Slutsky <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>p</mi></mover><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⋅</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. For normality write <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The summands <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> have mean 0 and variance <math display=\"inline\"><mi>B</mi></math>, so the CLT gives <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>d</mi></mover><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Normal</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>; apply Slutsky. ◻</p>\n</div>\n<p data-b=\"44\">The matrix <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>B</mi><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> is the <strong>sandwich</strong> (heteroskedasticity-robust) variance; it simplifies to <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> if <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h5 data-b=\"45\" id=\"b45\">When (i) fails: endogeneity.</h5>\n<p data-b=\"46\">For a single regressor, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">plim</mtext><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Cov</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>/</mi><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Var</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>. This is the whole motivation for instrumental variables: Chapter 20 replaces <math display=\"inline\"><mi>x</mi></math> in the moment condition by a variable <math display=\"inline\"><mi>z</mi></math> that is uncorrelated with <math display=\"inline\"><mi>u</mi></math>.</p>\n<h5 data-b=\"47\" id=\"b47\">Numerical check (mine).</h5>\n<p data-b=\"48\">Let <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></math> with <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></math> independent standard normals. Then <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Cov</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Var</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Var</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, so OLS converges to <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow></math>. In my simulation with <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> the OLS slope is 2.50 and the IV slope using <math display=\"inline\"><mi>z</mi></math> is 2.00, to two decimals.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"49\" id=\"b49\">Panel notation and the two basic transformations</h2>\n<p data-b=\"50\">Observations are indexed <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> (units: banks) and <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> (years). The workhorse model is <span class=\"math display\">$$y_{it}=x_{it}\\beta+c_i+u_{it},\n\\;\\;\\text{\\small(19.1)}$$</span> where <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math> is an <strong>unobserved effect</strong> (a bank’s permanent characteristics) that is allowed to be correlated with <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></math>, and <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></math> is the <strong>idiosyncratic error</strong>. Pooled OLS on (19.1) is inconsistent when <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Cov</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, since <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math> sits in the error.</p>\n<h5 data-b=\"51\" id=\"b51\">Within (fixed-effects) transformation.</h5>\n<p data-b=\"52\">Average (19.1) over <math display=\"inline\"><mi>t</mi></math>: <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>y</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mover><mi>u</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Subtract: <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>y</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mover><mi>u</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> with <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>y</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover><mi>y</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math> vanish. In matrix form the transformation is <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>ι</mi><mi>ι</mi><mi>′</mi></mrow></math> (with <math display=\"inline\"><mi>ι</mi></math> a vector of ones); <math display=\"inline\"><mi>Q</mi></math> is a projection matrix like <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub></math>, and OLS on the transformed data is <math display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mrow><mi>F</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo form=\"prefix\" maxsize=\"1.8\" minsize=\"1.8\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo form=\"postfix\" maxsize=\"1.8\" minsize=\"1.8\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>.</mi></mrow></math></p>\n<h5 data-b=\"53\" id=\"b53\">First differencing.</h5>\n<p data-b=\"54\">Subtract (19.1) at <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> from <math display=\"inline\"><mi>t</mi></math>: <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>Δ</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>Δ</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>.</p>\n<div class=\"box thm\">\n<p data-b=\"55\"><strong>Result 19.3 (When FE and FD are consistent).</strong> Suppose <em>strict exogeneity</em> holds: <math display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mspace width=\"0.222em\"></mspace><mrow><mspace width=\"0.333em\"></mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\"> for all </mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"></mspace></mrow><mi>t</mi><mi>.</mi></mrow></math> Then <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mi>′</mi><msub><mover><mi>u</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and FE is consistent (fixed <math display=\"inline\"><mi>T</mi></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>∞</mi></mrow></math>) provided <math display=\"block\"><mrow><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>t</mi></munder><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mi>′</mi><msub><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"></mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">has full rank.</mtext></mrow></math></p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"proof\">\n<p data-b=\"56\"><em>Proof.</em> <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>u</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></math> is a function of <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></math>. Strict exogeneity makes every <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></math> mean-independent of every <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></math>, so <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mi>′</mi><msub><mover><mi>u</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Then <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mrow><mi>F</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>′</mi><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mover><mo>→</mo><mi>p</mi></mover><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> by the WLLN. ◻</p>\n</div>\n<p data-b=\"57\"><em>Why this matters in Chapter 23:</em> a lagged dependent variable <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math> is <em>not</em> strictly exogenous: <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math> depends on <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math>, and the within transformation puts <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math> into <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mover><mi>u</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></math> through <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mover><mi>u</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></math>, and <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></math> into <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mover><mi>y</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math> through <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mover><mi>y</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></math>. The correlation that results is the Nickell bias.</p>\n<h2 data-b=\"58\" id=\"b58\">Check yourself</h2>\n<div class=\"qlist\">\n<p data-b=\"59\">State the WLLN, the CLT and Slutsky’s theorem in one line each.</p>\n<p data-b=\"60\">What are the defining properties of <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub></math>?</p>\n<p data-b=\"61\">Define strict exogeneity of <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></math> in model (19.1).</p>\n<p data-b=\"62\">Why is <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> weaker than <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>? Give a case where the first holds and the second does not.</p>\n<p data-b=\"63\">Why does fixed <math display=\"inline\"><mi>T</mi></math> matter for the choice of asymptotic framework?</p>\n<p data-b=\"64\">Why does the within transformation remove <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math> but not create a new problem when <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></math> is strictly exogenous?</p>\n<p data-b=\"65\">Show that <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">tr</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">rank</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> for <math display=\"inline\"><mi>Z</mi></math> with <math display=\"inline\"><mi>L</mi></math> columns, and say what this implies about the number of overidentifying restrictions in a regression of <math display=\"inline\"><mi>x</mi></math> on <math display=\"inline\"><mi>Z</mi></math>.</p>\n<p data-b=\"66\">Let <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></math> as in the example. Find the plim of OLS and of IV using <math display=\"inline\"><mi>z</mi></math> as instrument.</p>\n<p data-b=\"67\">Compute <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mrow><mi>F</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub></math> for <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> with data <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>21</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>22</mn></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>21</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>22</mn></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p data-b=\"68\">“Consistency is a large-sample property, so it is irrelevant to a panel of 12 banks.” Discuss.</p>\n<p data-b=\"69\">“FE and FD give the same estimate when <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.” Prove it, and say what changes for <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n</div>\n<h3 class=\"unnumbered\" data-b=\"70\" id=\"b70\">Answer sketches</h3>\n<div class=\"alist\">\n<p data-b=\"71\">WLLN: sample means converge in probability to expectations. CLT: standardised sums are asymptotically normal. Slutsky: continuous functions of convergent sequences converge to the function of the limit; products of <math display=\"inline\"><mi>p</mi></math>- and <math display=\"inline\"><mi>d</mi></math>-convergent sequences converge in distribution.</p>\n<p data-b=\"72\">Symmetric, idempotent; <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p data-b=\"73\"><math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for all <math display=\"inline\"><mi>t</mi></math>.</p>\n<p data-b=\"74\"><math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> implies zero correlation with every function of <math display=\"inline\"><mi>x</mi></math>; the converse fails: let <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> with <math display=\"inline\"><mi>x</mi></math> standard normal, so <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> but <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p data-b=\"75\">With <math display=\"inline\"><mi>T</mi></math> fixed there is no averaging over time to wash out <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mover><mi>u</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></math>; all consistency comes from <math display=\"inline\"><mi>N</mi></math>.</p>\n<p data-b=\"76\"><math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math> cancels exactly; the transformed error <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mover><mi>u</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></math> is uncorrelated with every <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></math> under strict exogeneity, so OLS on the transformed data is consistent.</p>\n<p data-b=\"77\">Trace of <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></math> is <math display=\"inline\"><mi>L</mi></math>, so <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">rank</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>; regressing <math display=\"inline\"><mi>x</mi></math> on <math display=\"inline\"><mi>Z</mi></math> uses <math display=\"inline\"><mi>L</mi></math> parameters; with <math display=\"inline\"><mi>K</mi></math> endogenous regressors the overidentification degree is <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p data-b=\"78\">OLS: <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow></math>. IV with <math display=\"inline\"><mi>z</mi></math>: <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Cov</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Cov</mtext><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so IV <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>→</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p data-b=\"79\">Unit 1: <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>y</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>y</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>. Unit 2: <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>y</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>2.5</mn></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>1.5</mn></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>y</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>. <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>∑</mo><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈</mo></mover><mover><mi>y</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>0.25</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0.25</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2.5</mn></mrow></math>; <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msup><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>0.25</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0.25</mn><mo form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2.5</mn></mrow></math>. So <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>β</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂</mo></mover><mrow><mi>F</mi><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p data-b=\"80\">Consistency tells you what the estimator is aiming at; it does not tell you the finite-sample error. With <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></math> the asymptotic standard errors can be very misleading (Chapters 24–26); consistency is necessary, not sufficient.</p>\n<p data-b=\"81\">For <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>: <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>y</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>Δ</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>y</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>Δ</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, so <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mi>y</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>Δ</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>Δ</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msubsup><mover><mi>x</mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>Δ</mi><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mi>/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>: the ratio is the FD estimator. For <math display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> they differ: FD uses only adjacent differences and is serially correlated if <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></math> is serially uncorrelated; FE is more efficient under serially uncorrelated <math display=\"inline\"><mi>u</mi></math>.</p>\n</div>\n","s":[[142,"b3"],[142,"b5"],[143,"b19"],[143,"b26"],[144,"b40"],[145,"b49"],[145,"b58"]]};